//kage:unit pixels

package main

// The one shape shader of gift.
//
// Every drawing operation of the display list is a rounded rectangle, filled
// or stroked, so exactly one shader and one draw call serve all of them. The
// project plan, section 8, requires analytic geometry in a shared shape
// shader and forbids an offscreen image per widget; this is that shader.
//
// Everything the shape needs travels in the vertex attributes, not in
// uniforms: uniforms are a map[string]any in Ebitengine's API and would
// allocate once per operation, which the project plan, section 11, does not
// allow in the frame path.
//
//	src0Pos  position inside the rectangle, 0..size, origin top left
//	color    premultiplied fill or stroke colour
//	custom.x half width
//	custom.y half height
//	custom.z corner radius, already clamped by the CPU side
//	custom.w stroke width; zero or less means fill
//
// # No screen space derivatives
//
// All five geometric values above arrive pre-scaled to device pixels. The CPU
// side knows the local-to-device scale of the transform — see the renderer's
// deviceScale — and bakes it into the half extents, the radius, the stroke
// width and the local position before it writes the vertex. The distance
// field below is therefore already measured in device pixels, so the width of
// the antialiasing band is the constant 1 and this shader needs no fwidth,
// dfdx or dfdy at all.
//
// That is deliberate, but the original reason for it turned out to be wrong
// and is recorded here so nobody reinstates it. The project plan once named
// derivatives as the highest-variance unknown of the project, on the theory
// that Ebitengine might fall back to GLSL ES 1.00 on a Raspberry Pi 4, where
// dfdx and dfdy need the OES_standard_derivatives extension. Ebitengine 2.10.1
// has no ES 1.00 path at all: internal/shaderir/glsl emits either #version 150
// or #version 300 es, and nothing else. Derivatives are core in GLSL ES 3.00.
//
// The rewrite is kept on its own merits, which are real. Coverage in device
// pixels fixed a latent bug in the vertex padding, which used to pad one
// *local* unit and therefore clipped the outer half of every antialiased edge
// under a downscale. It also removed the artefacts fwidth produced at the
// kinks of the distance field — the corner diagonals and the centre line of a
// stroke band — where a finite difference across a 2x2 fragment quad straddles
// a fold and blurs pixels that are genuinely fully covered. And it makes the
// output independent of how a driver lays out those quads.
//
// The actual Pi risk is failing to obtain an ES 3.0 context at all, which
// would break the whole renderer rather than just rounded corners, and which
// no shader rewrite can help with.

// sdRoundBox is the signed distance from p to a rounded box centred at the
// origin with half extents b and corner radius r. Negative inside.
func sdRoundBox(p vec2, b vec2, r float) float {
	q := abs(p) - b + vec2(r)
	return length(max(q, vec2(0))) + min(max(q.x, q.y), 0.0) - r
}

func Fragment(dstPos vec4, src0Pos vec2, color vec4, custom vec4) vec4 {
	half := custom.xy
	radius := custom.z
	stroke := custom.w

	// A plain axis aligned fill needs no distance field at all. The quad the
	// CPU side emitted is already exactly the rectangle, clipped, so shading
	// it uniformly is both cheaper and free of the seams an antialiased edge
	// would leave between two adjacent fills.
	if radius <= 0.0 {
		if stroke <= 0.0 {
			return color
		}
	}

	// The position arrives as 0..size so that no vertex attribute is ever
	// negative; the distance field wants it centred.
	p := src0Pos - half

	d := sdRoundBox(p, half, radius)

	if stroke > 0.0 {
		// The stroke lies inside the bounds: the band runs from -stroke to
		// 0, so recentring the distance on -stroke/2 turns it into a fill of
		// a band of that width. See the project plan, section 8.
		d = abs(d+stroke*0.5) - stroke*0.5
	}

	// d is in device pixels, so the coverage ramp is one pixel wide and
	// centred on the boundary. Scaling the whole vec4, colour and alpha
	// together, is what keeps the result premultiplied.
	return color * clamp(0.5-d, 0.0, 1.0)
}
